Done. Summary of the work:

## Fault located
`packages/coding-agent/src/core/resource-loader.ts`, in `DefaultResourceLoader.reload()`:

```ts
preTrustExtensions = await this.loadProjectTrustExtensions();
const projectTrusted = await options.resolveProjectTrust({ extensionsResult: preTrustExtensions });
this.settingsManager.setProjectTrusted(false);   // decision ignored
```

The bootstrap pass (`loadProjectTrustExtensions()`) correctly forces untrusted settings so project extensions/packages stay excluded, but the value returned by `resolveProjectTrust` was dropped and `false` re-applied. The subsequent `settingsManager.reload()` and `packageManager.resolve()` therefore always used the bootstrap trust state, so an explicit *trusted* decision never loaded project resources.

## Repair (3 lines changed, one production file)
```ts
const projectTrusted = await options.resolveProjectTrust({ extensionsResult: preTrustExtensions });
// Honor the resolved decision (true or false) so the final settings reload and
// resource resolution use the trust state the caller explicitly chose.
this.settingsManager.setProjectTrusted(projectTrusted);
```
This keeps bootstrap untrusted, honors both `true` and `false`, and lets the existing `settingsManager.reload()` run for the decided state before the final `packageManager.resolve()`.

## Regression (`.prale-eval/regression.mjs`)
Standalone, dependency-free `node:assert/strict` script driving the real loader through `.prale-eval/subject.mjs`. For `initialTrusted` in `{true,false}` and decisions `{true,false}` it asserts:
- bootstrap consults `resolveProjectTrust` with only `cli.ts`/`user.ts` and resolves with `loadedTrust === false`;
- the decision reaches the settings manager, settings are reloaded for it before the final resolve, and the final resolve carries `loadedTrust === decision`;
- `project.ts` is present only for a trusted decision, absent otherwise; the settings manager ends in the decided state.

## Validation
`node --experimental-vm-modules .prale-eval/regression.mjs` → exit 0. Negative control: reverting only that line makes the script fail with `AssertionError: final resource resolution ignored the explicit trust decision true: ["resolve",false]` (exit 1). Limitation recorded in the notes: no `node_modules` present, so `tsc`/`vitest` could not be run.

